Levator Ani Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic dull aching rectal pain, Exacerbation by prolonged sitting, Exquisite tenderness of levator ani on palpation, Normal anoscopy/pelvic MRI

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-031
Reason for Visit: Severe, intractable rectal pain causing absolute inability to sit and profound distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, escalating rectal pressure and pain causing functional immobility and extreme distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to perform a digital rectal examination to definitively rule out an acute perianal abscess, thrombosed hemorrhoid, or impacted stool, and to provide immediate pharmacological relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is an unbearable, heavy, dragging ache high up in my rectum; I can't sit down at all, it feels like something is trying to push out."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute 24-hour exacerbation of a chronic, recurrent dull aching pain strictly localized to the rectum. The pain is described as a severe sensation of pressure or extreme heaviness high in the pelvic floor. It is severely exacerbated by prolonged sitting at an office desk today, but the patient notes significant relief upon standing or lying completely flat. The pain briefly worsened following a normal bowel movement this evening. The pain is continuous and aching, not the sharp, fleeting pain of proctalgia fugax. Patient reports high occupational stress recently.
Functional Status: Pacing the room or lying supine; absolutely refuses to sit in a chair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound discomfort leading to severe anxiety and inability to rest.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, shifting weight constantly while standing.
Physical Exam: External perianal inspection is completely normal, lacking fissures, thrombosed external hemorrhoids, erythema, or fluctuant abscesses. Digital rectal examination reveals an empty rectal vault (no impaction). However, there is exquisite tenderness and a severe, hypertonic pulling sensation upon direct digital palpation of the levator ani muscle (specifically the puborectalis sling on the left side). The prostate (if applicable) is normal, and there is no palpable mass. Documented history of a completely normal anoscopy and pelvic MRI 4 months prior.
Environmental Observations: Patient's home office chair is rigid and lacks ergonomic pelvic support.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; simple analgesics ineffective.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Levator Ani Syndrome - Status: Acute hypertonic spasm flare
Differentials:

Perianal/Ischiorectal Abscess - Ruled out by the strictly normal temperature, completely normal external perianal inspection, and absence of a fluctuant, hot mass on DRE.

Thrombosed Hemorrhoid - Ruled out by the absence of a visible, exquisitely tender blue/purple perianal lump.

Proctalgia Fugax - Ruled out clinically as the pain is continuous and aching for hours, rather than a sharp, fleeting stab lasting only seconds to minutes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, intractable rectal pain and an absolute inability to sit, mimicking an acute structural anorectal emergency (such as a deep space abscess or acute thrombosis). Immediate physical examination, strictly including a digital rectal examination, was mandatory to definitively rule out sepsis or surgical pathology. By clinically localizing the exquisite pain specifically to a hypertonic levator ani muscle sling and confirming the absence of structural disease, the physician safely diagnosed a benign functional spasm, thereby preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary emergency department admission. This fulfills the strict criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Diazepam (2mg BID for 3 days) for targeted skeletal muscle relaxation of the pelvic floor. Advised a 20-minute hot sitz bath immediately to provide topical thermal relaxation.
Positive Behavioral Change: When working, implement a strict protocol of standing up and walking for 5 minutes every 45 minutes of sitting. Physically offloading the direct mechanical pressure on the pelvic floor prevents the progressive accumulation of muscle tension, directly rewarding the patient with a sustained reduction in the heavy, aching sensation and reinforcing the necessity of taking breaks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist for biofeedback therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Donut cushion or coccyx cut-out cushion for the desk chair.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to assess response to muscle relaxants.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever with chills, sudden localized swelling or redness around the anus, passage of large amounts of blood, or absolute inability to pass urine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes previously normal pelvic MRI remains accurate; if fevers develop, deep pelvic infection must be immediately excluded.
Clinical Course: Levator ani syndrome is a chronic tension headache of the pelvic floor muscles. It is intensely uncomfortable but structurally benign. The spasms will fluctuate with stress and sitting habits but can be managed with physiotherapy and muscle relaxation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a fever, notice a hot, swollen, painful lump near your anus, or if you completely stop being able to urinate.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles that form a hammock at the bottom of your pelvis are currently in a severe, continuous cramp. Because the muscle is physically tight, sitting directly on it causes agonizing pressure. Muscle relaxants and heat will break the cramp; antibiotics or surgery are not needed.
Actionable Steps: Take a hot bath tonight, immersing your pelvis completely. The deep, ambient heat will mechanically increase blood flow to the spastic muscle and physically force it to relax, providing much faster and more effective relief than standard painkillers.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Levator Ani Syndrome (pelvic muscle spasm). Exam shows NO abscess or hemorrhoid. Take prescribed muscle relaxant and use a hot bath. See GP in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

